        "provenance": "inferred",
        "spec": "Caller builds a Graph via GraphBuilder — interning string ids, supplying finite f64 weights and a directed flag — and results come back keyed by dense node index for the caller to map back. No storage, service or core dependency.",
        "transport_security": "unspecified"
      }
    },
    {
      "node_type": "Interface",
      "node_id": "ifc:kv-backend",
      "properties": {
        "auth": "none",
        "designation": "published",
        "medium": "library",
        "name": "KvBackend trait (storage seam)",
        "operations": "six byte-level primitives over six named column families, plus ordered prefix iteration",
        "paradigm": "synchronous",
        "payload_format": "binary",
        "provenance": "inferred",
        "spec": "The narrow seam underneath StorageEngine. All schema-aware behaviour — validation, the reverse index, the read cache, the write-buffer overlay, the full-text sidecar — sits ABOVE this trait; everything that touches bytes goes through it. Ordered prefix iteration is the one structural assumption every backend must honour, because the adjacency and index scans are built on it. An EXTENSION POINT: implementing it is how a new backend (Postgres, sled) arrives without the engine changing.",
        "transport_security": "none"
      }
    },
    {
